diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-layer investigation found no fault on any device in the simple_bgp topology. Phase 1 reachability testing showed both pc1→pc2 and pc2→pc1 paths completing with 0% packet loss. All Phase A (L2/infrastructure), Phase B (BGP control plane), and Phase D (service/resource pressure) triage helpers returned clean results with no flagged devices.

Targeted BGP inspection confirmed full routing symmetry: router1 (AS1) has a valid, FIB-installed BGP route `200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2` learned from AS2, and router2 (AS2) has a valid, FIB-installed BGP route `195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1` learned from AS1. Both BGP sessions are in the Established state, both routers are advertising their respective /24 prefixes, and no route-maps, inbound/outbound filters, or blackhole routes exist on either router. The BGP RIB on each device shows exactly two valid best-path routes with no RIB-failure or Stale entries. The observed RTT asymmetry (~0.47 ms vs ~1.03 ms) falls within normal container-scheduling variation for a Kathara virtual lab and is not a fault indicator.

Root cause: No fault is present. The return path from AS2 to AS1 is fully operational and correctly routed via BGP.

Fix: No corrective action is required. The user's perceived asymmetric-routing issue cannot be reproduced or confirmed by any live evidence; the network is functioning as designed.
